From: duncan0k <dev@ark0.si>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:02:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 103/105] test: add feature_p2mr_lattice_tripwire.py,
 feature_p2mr_lattice_tripwire_signet.py and
 feature_p2mr_lattice_tripwire_storage.py

feature_p2mr_lattice_tripwire.py runs four regtest nodes with the
tripwire of leaf version 0xe0 and three lattice tripwire outputs of the
shape the Ark-0 ones will have (L1 with the NUMS key, L2 and L3 with keys
the test holds): one with the default policy, one with -acceptnonstdtxn=1,
an M4-R node without the lattice rules, and one whose rescue lane opens
above the lattice height. It covers the activation boundary in the mempool
and in blocks, the 80-byte limit at depth zero, a reorganization below
the height; the block that spends a lattice tripwire output (ML-DSA-44
spends of leaf version 0xe2 before and after the spend in it are valid,
the same spends one block later are not, in the mempool, in a block and
in a proposal, past both caches, while the M4-R node takes that block); a
spend only in the mempool; removal from the mempool on a plain connect
(chains, a package, TRUC, mixed inputs, a child of two removed ML-DSA-44
spends next to an SLH-DSA-only branch, with the exact mempool) and after a
reorganization (the same families, with the exact mempool again), with
a parent that comes back while its ML-DSA-44 child goes and its SLH-DSA
child stays, and a reorganization whose first block repeats the
transactions of the block it replaces, so that the mempool is empty when
the trigger connects and the node checks it after the reorganization
without stopping; reorganizations over the trigger and one that brings back
one of two missing outputs; the tripwire of leaf version 0xe0 and a
lattice tripwire spent in one block and in adjacent blocks, with the
mempool checked after each; a lattice tripwire spent below a lattice
height given later, so that the first block at that height refuses an
ML-DSA-44 spend of leaf version 0xe2; the outputs moved by a
reorganization and confirmed again at the height, which leaves them
missing; regtest's default height following OP_CHECKMLDSA44, so that a
reorganization below it removes a spend of leaf version 0xe2; both tripwires missing (Schnorr, S2, an
ML-DSA-44 AND SLH-DSA leaf, SLH1); the NUMS output; the M4-R lane in leaf
version 0xe2 (an elliptic curve rescue with a real proof valid in a block
from the higher of the two heights, on a node whose lane opens below the
lattice height and on one whose lane opens above it, not standard, not
logged as template R1; a rescue token in place of an ML-DSA-44 signature
refused); the logs and gettripwireinfo; VerifyDB level 4, restarts,
-reindex-chainstate, -reindex; a late upgrade of the M4-R node, whose
state is right once it has the rules and which keeps a block that breaks
them until -reindex; a spendable output in the first lattice slot, which
triggers as the others do (block X with ML-DSA-44 spends around the
trigger, block X+1 refusing one and taking an SLH-DSA spend); an outpoint
that was never created, with a block holding an ML-DSA-44 spend of leaf
version 0xe2 refused and one holding an SLH-DSA spend taken; and the
options.

feature_p2mr_lattice_tripwire_signet.py runs a custom signet with
-signetlatticetripwire, whose lattice tripwire output never existed, and
an Ark-0 node: on the first the rules apply and disable ML-DSA-44 checks
from their height, on Ark-0 they are not active, the deployment is not
listed and the node says so at startup.

feature_p2mr_lattice_tripwire_storage.py does for leaf version 0xe2 what
feature_p2mr_tripwire.py does for 0xe0 with assumeutxo and pruning: a
node loads the regtest snapshot at 299 with a lattice tripwire output
that block 200 spends, so that the snapshot chainstate has it missing and
refuses an ML-DSA-44 spend of 0xe2 from the first block after the
snapshot: in the mempool before block 300, and in a candidate block 300
with its funding, while block 300 with the funding and an SLH-DSA spend is
taken; and again at 301, on confirmed outputs; the
background chainstate replays block 200 without logging the spend, and
after it and after a restart the state and the refusals are the same. A
pruned node prunes, connects a trigger, drops the ML-DSA-44 spend from
its mempool, goes back over the trigger inside the blocks it keeps and
forward again; once the trigger's block is pruned it rebuilds the same
state at restart, refuses and takes the same spends, and reorganizes
above its prune height, over a second trigger too.

test_framework/p2mr_lattice_tripwire.py computes the NUMS key with
Python's hashlib, independently of the unit test, and builds the trees
and witnesses.
